WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:09.400
LORRAINE CINK: Marvel
Studios' The Falcon

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.930
and the Winter
Soldier no doubt has

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:14.650
some of the most high-flying
VFX in the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.840
Universe, which is
why I wanted to talk

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.590
to VFX supervisor, Eric Leven.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:19.390
Hey, Eric.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.190
 Hi, Lorraine.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.760
LORRAINE CINK: Now the series
has some incredible action

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:23.560
sequences.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.140
What was your overall
vision for those action

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.300
sequences in the show?

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.200
ERIC LEVEN: What I really
wanted to do for the big action

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.240
sequences was, as
much as possible,

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.330
make things feel like
they're grounded in reality.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.360
So obviously, we don't have
Anthony Mackie flying around

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.100
in a real jetpack
with real wings,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.350
but we want to make it
feel like, if he was,

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.550
how would we film that?

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.440
There are people who
can actually fly.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.780
You have wingsuit pilots
who jump out of airplanes.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.490
They all have little
helmet cameras.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.260
Kari Skogland, the
director, really

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.580
wanted to bring that
language to our show as well.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.620
So we actually have many shots
where we've got an action cam

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.790
in front of Sam's face, or on
the wingtip, and as he moves,

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.660
we see the entire world
move around him and things

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:03.460
like that.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.710
And that was a really
interesting, new

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.120
visual language to
bring to the show

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.470
that we haven't brought before.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.660
 I think that new visual
language is really clear,

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.520
especially when you
look at other MCU

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.710
characters who also fly.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:17.200
But what makes Falcon's
flight so unique?

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.940
ERIC LEVEN: It was
important for us

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.010
to have Falcon feel like
a different character.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.690
There are so many other
characters in the MCU

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.450
that fly, like Iron
Man, for example.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.250
He flies, but he has
a different center

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.590
of gravity because his
engines are in a different

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.510
place than Falcon's.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.700
He can hover in
a way that Falcon

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.090
doesn't necessarily hover.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.200
So we wanted to make sure
that Falcon felt different

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.300
and that we weren't sort
of copying the flight

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.790
patterns of other heroes.

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:44.680
 Well, let's talk
about where we really

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.570
see this happening
in the show, which

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.820
is that big opening sequence.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.620
 It opens the show, so you're
sort of dropped kind of right

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.420
in the middle of it.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.920
Sam is kind of contracting
with the Air Force.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.320
There's a terrorist
organization out there

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.270
that is-- they think
is going to kidnap

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.260
an American Air Force plane.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.447
And so they're sending
Sam in to go make contact

00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:03.880
with that plane, make
sure everything's OK,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.200
they've lost contact with it.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.360
Then what he doesn't know,
is that these guys are

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.729
expert wingsuit pilots
and they actually

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:13.090
strap Captain Vassant to them
and then jump out of the plane.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.920
So it was just sort of
this amazing moment,

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.670
like I didn't know
they could do that.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:20.060
So now Sam has to chase
after these wingsuit guys.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.960
 So the first thing that we
see is Sam in the airplane,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.580
and then he drops out backwards.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.710
How did you create that shot?

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.320
 Yeah, that was a relatively
straightforward shot.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.850
We've seen that sort
of thing done before,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.280
so we wanted to make sure
we put our stamp on it.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.020
Basically, he's
in the real plane.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.370
There are pads below him.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.070
There's a blue screen
surrounding the plane.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:40.660
And then when he falls
out of the plane,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.640
somewhere in the
middle of that process

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.310
we basically blend to a
digital version of him.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:46.720
Ideally, the effect is seamless.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.840
You don't know where
you know Anthony starts

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.970
and where the digital
version begins.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.980
 That sequence is so wild.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.930
You really can't
tell what's real

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.580
and what was shot practically.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.560
How did you shoot
the scene where

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.660
Falcon lands on the plane?

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.310
Is that shot in the air?

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.740
 Not with a plane in the air,
but with a plane on the ground.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.390
That was something that we--

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.480
we previs that.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.600
So we saw like, the sort of
video game version of it,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.880
where we had Falcon
kind of coming

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.370
in and just sort of landing
on the plane's cockpit.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.100
And we talked with the stunt
coordinator and the riggers

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.560
and figured out, you know, I
think we can do that for real.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.970
We're going to be in
an Air Force base.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.370
We have a real plane.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.730
And they would wire
Anthony up on a traveling

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.610
harness that would actually
fly him into the shot.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.600
And Anthony really did it.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.080
He actually flew onto
the nose of an aircraft.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.790
In that particular shot, I think
we've replaced him completely

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.490
at the beginning of the
shot because we didn't like

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:41.320
the trajectory at the
beginning, and then

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.970
we blended to real
Anthony landing

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.460
on the nose of the aircraft.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.220
 So the next crazy
thing that happens

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.020
is, there are these
wingsuit flyers

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.640
weaving between the canyons.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.710
Did you work with
real wingsuits?

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:56.190
 Not only are we working with
actual folks in wingsuits,

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.930
but Captain Vassant is strapped
to one of the wingsuit .

00:03:58.930 --> 00:03:59.730
pilots.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:00.730
 In real life?

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:01.530
 Yeah.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.510
And so, that was
the sort of thing--

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.400
you know, in the story he's
strapped-- in the story,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.350
yeah, he's going to strap him
and he's going to jump out.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.130
And I was talking with
the stunt coordinator,

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.212
and he said, Oh, yeah,
yeah, we can do that.

00:04:11.212 --> 00:04:12.070
What?
- What?

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:12.870
 Oh, yeah.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.340
No, we do it all the time.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.089
You just strap a
guy to another guy

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:17.220
and he'll just
hold on real tight

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.510
and they'll jump
out of the airplane.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.390
Any time you can do
anything for real,

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:22.530
you want to do it for real.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.090
I think what's
really important in--

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.290
especially in a
sequence like this--

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.200
is even if, I don't know,
70%, 80% of the sequence

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.470
is done in a computer, it's
CG, but having enough of it

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.230
to know that it's real and
to be able to sell people,

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.280
Oh, they did that for real.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.250
That's a real guy jumping out
of an airplane with someone

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:38.050
strapped to his back.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.380
If you have enough shots
in there that are real,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.550
I think it kind
of makes the rest

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:42.450
of the sequence believable.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.260
Then you start to question
what's real, what's not,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.060
what's digital.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.870
And if you can't
tell, that's the goal.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.010
And then it seems like maybe
it was all done for real.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.270
LORRAINE CINK: The show is
action-packed from start

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.070
to finish.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.950
And as we arrive at
the series finale,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:01.050
we finally see Sam's heroic
debut as Captain America.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.750
Could you talk us through
that big final scene?

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.080
 Yeah, so everything kind of
comes to a head at the end.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.350
And our last episode, the
Flag-Smashers have basically--

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.390
they've sort of become
radicalized at this point.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.970
Basically, they just
take a bunch of hostages.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.310
So one of the
Flag-Smashers takes a bunch

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.810
of hostages in a
helicopter and is flying

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.130
them all over New York City.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.660
Then, there are two
other trucks that are

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:23.400
hijacked by the Flag-Smashers.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.450
So everybody converges here.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.360
And at one point, Karli is
just like, You know what?

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.530
I can't take this anymore.

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.800
And she's going to take one
of the trucks full of hostages

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.020
and just drive it into
a construction pit.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.120
And it looks like the truck
is going to hit the ground,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.120
but at the last
second, Captain America

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.860
shows up and saves the day.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.430
I remember hearing from
Malcolm Spellman, the writer,

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.140
that he really wanted to make
this iconic moment of Sam

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.960
as Captain America saving
this truckload of people

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.123
and pushing it back to safety.

00:05:53.123 --> 00:05:54.390
That that was really
important to him.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.670
To have a Black
superhero do that was

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.130
really a powerful moment.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.860
 It really is such
an impactful moment.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.970
How are you able to bring
it to life with VFX?

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:05.370
ERIC LEVEN: It's sort
of the same process.

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.720
Again, it starts with the page.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.870
We'll start talking about, OK,
let's look at some storyboards.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.170
What are the shots
going to look like?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:11.970
Are these wide shots?

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.140
Are they tight shots?

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.730
If they're tight
shots, maybe we can

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.140
do it on a blue screen stage.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.730
Can we get one of these trucks?

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.490
Can we get it in a way that
Anthony Mackie can actually

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.260
get his body into it
and get the performance

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.170
that we were looking for.

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.620
And so Special Effects
actually came in and rigged up

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.480
one of these giant
BearCats in a way

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.030
that the Mackie could actually
push against and pretend

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:30.960
he was pushing it up.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.450
We start figuring
out what can be done

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:34.980
for real from all
the other departments

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.060
who say what they can do.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.435
The pit itself, for
example, was shot over a--

00:06:39.435 --> 00:06:41.640
I think it was three
story parking garage.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.160
And three stories doesn't
seem that dangerous

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:44.980
when you're at the top.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.160
You want to make sure that if
you fall in there, that there's

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.610
no chance of survival.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.480
So we might be able to film
at this practical location,

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.400
but we might need to take
it over in visual effects

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.150
and make it seem deeper, so
we'll do some set extensions.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:00.660
 It's definitely an absolutely
amazing moment in the show,

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:01.890
but I'm curious.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.570
Do you think there
are any moments

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.430
that people might have missed?

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.900
 What folks certainly missed
is that the outfit that Captain

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.490
America wears--
he has this cowl,

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.080
that I know everybody
at Marvel was very

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.790
specific about because
it's based on the look

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:15.900
at the cowl in the books.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.120
What I learned from
the costume department,

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.210
though, is that what they
want is really, really

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.043
hard to get in real life.

00:07:22.043 --> 00:07:23.377
I don't think there
are any fabrics that

00:07:23.377 --> 00:07:24.630
can do exactly what
they want, which

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.140
is it needs to be stretchy.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:27.570
It needs to hug the
face, but it also

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:29.220
has to feel thick
like leather, but it

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.200
needs to flex like Spandex.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.870
So what they end up doing
was they built something

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:35.280
as close as they
could possibly get it

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.830
and then depending how
Anthony turned his head,

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.930
you might get a huge
wrinkle back here.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.790
Or the cowl might
separate from his face.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.370
So in many, many
shots, we actually

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.920
replace that cowl so that it
would stay snug in his face

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.360
and look the way
we want it to look.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.520
There are seams in
the back of the cowl.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:54.030
And as we know, there are no
there are no seams or zippers

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.520
in the superhero
costume, so those all had

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.560
to be replaced and touched up.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.820
 There are so many details
that go into creating the VFX

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.130
for a series like
this, but what was

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.760
your absolute favorite aspect
of working on the series?

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.560
 Certainly, working
with these huge action

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.890
sequences was my favorite
part of the show.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:16.020
Because there's just so much
that goes into this stuff.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.550
There's so many moving parts.

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.210
You get to work with so
many different people,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:20.490
in so many different
departments,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.740
and bringing everybody's
creativity on the table,

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.570
and working with
these amazing people

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.210
to make these action sequences
better, and better, and better,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.860
was really, really fantastic.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.150
Some of these
scenes, we're working

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:31.110
on them for over a year.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.610
And it was just
really neat to see

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.230
them from the very
beginning all the way

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.429
through to the very end.

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:36.240
 Wow, Eric.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.320
It's been quite a ride.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.330
Thank you so much
for talking to us.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.480
And everybody, be sure to go
watch all episodes of Marvel

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.890
Studios' The Falcon
and the Winter Soldier,

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.910
now streaming on Disney Plus.

